{
  // ==========================================================================
  //  tsconfig.typecheck.nodenext.json — THE SHIPPED DECLARATION'S
  //  REACHABILITY UNDER A MODERN RESOLVER (`DEF-2026-10-16-Q`'s instrument,
  //  second leg)
  // ==========================================================================
  //  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The first leg (`tsconfig.typecheck.json`) checks the
  //  declaration under `moduleResolution: "node10"` — the mode that reads a
  //  package's top-level `types` field. A MODERN CONSUMER does not: under
  //  `nodenext` (and `bundler`) resolution walks the `exports` map, and the
  //  top-level `types` field is NOT consulted when an `exports` map is present.
  //  So a manifest whose `exports` map carries no `types` condition ships a
  //  declaration that a modern consumer CANNOT REACH — it silently receives
  //  `any` — while the `node10` leg stays GREEN.
  //
  //  WHAT THIS LEG MEASURES, AND IT MEASURES IT THE WAY A CONSUMER DOES. The
  //  SAME fixture (`typecheck/consumer-fixture.ts`), the SAME package-specifier
  //  import (`from 'provident-storeroom'`), the SAME strict/noEmit/`types: []`
  //  settings — with ONE setting moved: `moduleResolution: "nodenext"` (and
  //  `module: "nodenext"`, which TypeScript requires to accompany it). The
  //  fixture's `@ts-expect-error` refusals are untouched: they are re-checked here
  //  exactly as they are on the first leg, because an unused `@ts-expect-error` is
  //  itself a compile error. A run of this leg therefore proves TWO things at once —
  //  the declaration RESOLVES through the `exports` map, and every call the
  //  declaration must refuse is still refused.
  //
  //  ⟶ THE REFUSAL COUNT IS RE-TAKEN, 2026-11-01 (`Q-3`; `docs/L1/repair-outcome-types.md` §5.3). **THIS
  //  HEAD PRINTED `28` AND WAS WRONG BY TWO: read at the fixture's own `^\s*// @ts-expect-error` lines the
  //  directives stood at `26` before the repair-outcome cells landed.** **THEY STAND AT `43` NOW** — the pass
  //  that added §5.2's cells re-took the figure with them (§5.1's two re-derived cells, §5.2 (i)–(iv)'s
  //  assignability, refusal, census, narrowing and arity cells, and the `Q-1` clause's `CommitCarrier`
  //  cells). The reading is a plain count of that pattern in `typecheck/consumer-fixture.ts`, so it is
  //  falsifiable rather than trusted.
  //
  //  THE FAILURE MODE THIS LEG CATCHES, NAMED SO IT IS RECOGNISED RATHER THAN
  //  RE-DISCOVERED — `TS7016`: *"Could not find a declaration file for module
  //  'provident-storeroom'. '…/typecheck/node_modules/provident-storeroom/
  //  index.mjs' implicitly has an 'any' type. There are types at '…/index.d.ts',
  //  but this result could not be resolved when respecting package.json
  //  exports."* The instrument that found this was the FIRST leg; it could not
  //  have, because `node10` never reads the `exports` map.
  //
  //  ⟶ RE-DERIVED 2026-11-01 — `Q-3` IS TAKEN (§8's `Q-3`; settled by the supervisor the same date: YES, the
  //  pass that re-derives the cells corrects these two heads). **THE `TS7016` PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS THE READING OF
  //  ITS OWN DATE: the failure it names is the one a manifest with NO `types` CONDITION INSIDE ITS `exports` MAP
  //  produces, and that manifest is no longer this package's.** `bindings/js/package.json`'s `exports` map now
  //  carries `"types": "./index.d.ts"` beside `"import": "./index.mjs"`, so **THIS LEG'S SUBJECT HAS CHANGED FROM
  //  "CAN A MODERN CONSUMER REACH THE DECLARATION AT ALL?" TO "DOES THE `exports`-MAP ROUTING RESOLVE THE SAME
  //  DECLARATION THE CLASSIC ROUTING DOES, WITH EVERY REFUSAL STILL LIVE?"** — which is what a green here now
  //  proves, both halves, and the `TS7016` message stands above as the class of failure the leg still CATCHES if
  //  the `types` condition is ever removed from the map. **MEASURED, NOT INFERRED: the leg resolves
  //  `provident-storeroom` to `index.d.ts` and exits `0` on this tree, and so does the `node10` leg.**
  //
  //  THE TWO LEGS ARE ONE INSTRUMENT AND NEITHER REPLACES THE OTHER: `node10`
  //  is the routing the package also declares (its top-level `types` field, kept
  //  for classic consumers), and `nodenext` is the routing a modern consumer
  //  takes. Both must be green, and `npm run typecheck` runs both.
  // ==========================================================================
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    // THE MODERN RESOLVER: walks `exports`, ignores the top-level `types` field
    // when an `exports` map stands. `nodenext` is the stricter of the two
    // modern modes — `bundler` shares this map's resolution rules but is more
    // permissive about extensionless specifiers — so the failing mode is taken.
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "target": "es2022",
    "lib": ["es2022"],
    "types": [],
    "skipLibCheck": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  "include": ["typecheck/**/*.ts"]
}
